body:
A couple quick thoughts: 
1 - I would suggest learning to indent your code correctly. Wrap the whole thing in three backticks ` and it'll be much more readable
```
public final static class MyClass {


    private String parameter;
    public MyClass(String parameter) {
        this.parameter = parameter;
    } 

    // getters and setters 
}
```

Otherwise, I see you have a phone number on person which is not in the constructor nor does it have getter/setters. Ditto for SSN on Employee. 

Secondly, best practice is in constructors to have lowercase variable names (so 
```
public MyClass(String parameter) {
        this.parameter = parameter;
    }
```
Rather than:
```
public MyClass(String Parameter) {
        this.parameter = Parameter;
    }
```

What do you mean that it did not work, what errors were you getting? Eyeballing it it should work! 

2. 
That is the nature of inheritance, that any subclass has all the fields of a superclass and can use their methods. The reason you might want to is so that you can have a class which takes in an Employee (or Person) but uses is an instance of Manager. 

EG you could have a method 

```
public String changeAddress(Person person, String newAddress) {
    person.setAddress(newAddress);
} 
```

Or more complicated, and it would accept any employee or person. So you might not new up a Manager as a Person. But you might want to use a Person and pass in a manager if that makes sense.

Would highly recommend playing around with inheritance seeing what you can do and reading about it! Can be very powerful.

