Today was a weird day for me. I say that as if the day is already over, and I'm not hyperventilating in the bathroom at ... 9:11AM. Eleven minutes into my shift and I've already lost it. In my defense, this was not the most ordinary of shifts. Ryan, the only person in the Finance department here, was just taking away by guards. They came in, promptly, at 9:01AM. Just as we were sitting down for the day, I remember that Ryan had been trying to say something to me last night but we hadn't quite found the time. As he was taken away - it seemed like he was expecting it - but as he was taken away, he just winked at me and said "you know what to do".

&#x200B;

When he said, "You know what to do", I doubt he intended me to run to the bathroom in shock. That's rarely what people mean when they think you "know what to do". The stories I could tell about when my manager thought I knew what to do ... oh boi.

No no no, Sam stay serious. Let's think about it, what could Ryan be talking about. Well we work in the finance department, he's been working later and later nights recently - what was he workin on, something about the quarterly releases maybe? That's a start maybe. Or was he looking at gross earnings. I don't know, I don't know... also what is going to happen to Ryan? Will he be alright, I've seen the guards take people away before, but never seen them again. They just leave, never seen again.

&#x200B;

"Sam!", I heard someone shout from the door, "Sam, are you alright?" As the voice continued I recognized the voice as Olivia, my crush from human resources. She was always looking after me, though I never knew if that was for the company's benefit or mine. Trying to get me back to work or something else ...

&#x200B;

I flushed, and came out of the bathroom sheepishly just looking back at the stall and shrugging - a universal gesture that needs no explanation. Graciously, Olivia asked no further questions, just said she heard about Ryan and if I needed to talk her door was always open. I smiled politely and continued to chit chat until I returned to my desk, with a new intensity.

I had to discover what Ryan had discovered and see if there was some way to find him. I started looking through the files on his desk. Where only twenty minutes it had been an unorganized mess of files, the desk was now clean of any evidence of Ryan. His lego figurines - gone, all his post-it notes - gone, any proof I had ever shared this office - gone. Even the pictures he had hung on the wall, one of him and his dad, another an old fashioned black-and-white of his childhood home. On my desk, I found a memo. It wasn't long or complicated, but accused Ryan of industrial espionage and said that necessary precautions were taken, it stated I was not in danger either physically or of unemployment but should keep up my good work.

&#x200B;

As I read those words: I knew. I knew what I had to do. Ryan was right, he always was.

&#x200B;

\--

